    82	# included minutes on Aug 11 and hitting the $10 spending limit on Aug 14, which blocked Actions
    83	# outright. Projection at that cadence was ~$47/mo gross. We do not pay that for an internal tool.
    84	#
    85	# THE BRIDGE HAS ENDED. The re-arm keeps the constraints the private-phase analysis established:
    86	#   1. `push:` and `pull_request:` cover main and development. The macOS job itself remains guarded
    87	#      to a push on main; GH-347 moved the Ubuntu portability canary to push-on-development plus
    88	#      deliberate dispatch, so pull requests complete without paying for an advisory full suite.
    89	#   2. Expect a BATCH of accumulated Linux portability drift on the first green-field run. The local
    90	#      gate is macOS, so the platform we ship to stayed covered — the ubuntu canary did not, and drift
    91	#      has been accumulating unreported since this date. That backlog is the known, accepted cost of
    92	#      this decision, not a surprise.
    93	#   3. The GH-509 macOS boundary is again the promotion witness for an exact main commit.
    94	#
    95	# EVERYTHING BELOW IS DELIBERATELY PRESERVED — jobs, routing, and especially the GH-509 Phase 4 cost
    96	# analysis in the macOS job's header. That reasoning is what you will want when re-arming; deleting it
    97	# and rediscovering it on an invoice is the failure this comment exists to prevent.
    98	on:
    99	  push:
   100	    branches: [main, development]
   101	  pull_request:
   102	    branches: [main, development]
   103	  workflow_dispatch:
   104	
   105	concurrency:
   106	  # Cancel superseded runs within the same event lane. Keep manual integration runs
   107	  # independent so a branch push cannot cancel an operator-requested full gate.
   108	  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.event_name }}-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.ref }}
   109	  cancel-in-progress: true
   110	
   111	permissions:
   112	  contents: read
   113	
   114	jobs:
   115	  # GH-509 Phase 4 — THE PROMOTION BOUNDARY. This is the only job whose green means anything about
   116	  # what we ship, because it is the only one running the platform we ship to.
   117	  #
   118	  # WHY IT IS RARE, AND MUST STAY RARE: hosted macOS runners bill at roughly 10x Linux. At the volume
   119	  # this repo generates (37 pushes to `development` in one 24h sample) macOS-everywhere would cost
   120	  # thousands a month. Do NOT add `pull_request` here.
   121	  #
   122	  # WHY `workflow_dispatch` WAS REMOVED (2026-08-13, measured — this was the original design and it
   123	  # did not survive contact with the invoice). The theory was that a dispatch costs "well under a
   124	  # dollar a handful of times a month". The org's actual August usage says otherwise: macOS 3-core
   125	  # 75 minutes = **$4.65 net**, against Ubuntu's 2,740 minutes = $4.44 net. Seventy-five macOS minutes
   126	  # cost more than 2,740 Linux minutes, because macOS bills ~10x AND draws no included-minute discount
   127	  # on a private repo. Three dispatches on one branch in one day consumed roughly half a $10 monthly
   128	  # budget. The gating was correct; what was missing was any brake on how often the deliberate trigger
   129	  # gets pulled — and "deliberate" is not a rate limit when the same person can dispatch it while
   130	  # iterating on the workflow itself.
   131	  #
   132	  # WHAT THIS COSTS US, STATED PLAINLY: the original argument for `workflow_dispatch` was real —
   133	  # work lands on `development` and `main` sees almost nothing, so a main-only boundary arrives AFTER
   134	  # the promotion decision rather than before it. That gap is now open. The substitute is the LOCAL
   135	  # gate on the platform we ship to: `./validate.sh` (and, per GH-528, `--parallel N` for a ~3-minute
   136	  # run) plus `utils/gate-record.sh`, which is self-reported and does NOT qualify a promotion. If a
   137	  # pre-merge macOS witness is needed for a specific commit, re-enable this trigger deliberately,
   138	  # spend the ~$1.25-1.50, and turn it back off — rather than leaving it armed by default.
   139	  #
   140	  # HONEST LIMIT ON "EXACT COMMIT" (retained; applies to any re-enabled dispatch): `workflow_dispatch`
   141	  # targets a REF, not an arbitrary SHA — GitHub resolves the ref's current HEAD. So this job cannot
   142	  # be pointed at an old commit; it qualifies whatever that ref points to when dispatched. The resolved
   143	  # SHA is printed into the job summary for exactly this reason: the promotion rule compares against a
   144	  # recorded SHA, not against "the run I remember starting".
   145	  #
   146	  # NO SKIP LIST, deliberately. It invokes the authoritative validator directly rather than scraping
   147	  # `TESTS` out of it — the scrape can only see `.sh` entries, which is how the 20-test Python layer
   148	  # (the AUTHORITATIVE implementation since GH-264) went unexercised in CI for months. A second list
   149	  # is a second thing to keep honest; there is no second list here.
   150	  boundary-macos:
   151	    name: promotion boundary (macOS — the platform we ship to)
   152	    if: github.event_name == 'push' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
   153	    runs-on: macos-latest
   154	    # A bound, not an estimate: an unbounded hang is the expensive failure mode, so cap it rather than
   155	    # discover the cost afterwards. The cap tracks MEASURED hosted runtime, not the August "~13-15 min
   156	    # locally" (GH-823): wave-reconcile.yml ran this same `validate.sh --sequential` on this same runner
   157	    # in 60-92 min per job (2026-09-21..25) for every non-docs landing; since GH-1127 it is dispatched
   158	    # once before promotion and runs `--parallel 4` (~22 min, #1134), so the old 45-min cap would fail every promotion. Keep it >=
   159	    # that workflow's cap; test/ci-workflow.sh fails if it drops below.
   160	    timeout-minutes: 120
   161	    steps:
   162	      - name: Check out repo
   163	        uses: actions/checkout@v7
   164	        with:
   165	          # Same reason as the canary job: fixtures synthesize an "older" ancestor with
   166	          # `git rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD`, which a shallow clone silently defeats.
   167	          fetch-depth: 0
   168	
   169	      - name: Prepare git environment for fixture-driven tests
   170	        run: |
   171	          set -euo pipefail
   172	          git config --global init.defaultBranch main
   173	          git config --global user.email "ci@runner.invalid"
   174	          git config --global user.name "CI Runner"
   175	
   176	      - name: Install npm dependencies
   177	        run: npm ci
   178	
   179	      # The hosted runner's Python has no pytest, and validate.sh's python lane needs it — the
   180	      # first boundary dispatch (run 31661285957) failed exactly here. Ironic and instructive:
   181	      # GH-509's own headline defect was "the hosted route never ran the 20 python tests"; the
   182	      # first time they ran on hosted macOS, the environment couldn't run them.
   183	      - name: Install Python dependencies for the full suite
   184	        run: python3 -m pip install --quiet --break-system-packages -r requirements-dev.txt
   185	
   186	      - name: Verify automatic wave reconciliation (GH-421)
   187	        run: bash test/gh421-auto-wave-reconcile.sh
   188	
   189	      - name: Run the authoritative validator (no skips)
   190	        env:
   191	          # The one documented exclusion, and it is not a coverage skip: this test makes a real
   192	          # billed API call to a live agent. It self-skips off-PATH anyway; the env var makes the
   193	          # intent explicit rather than incidental.
   194	          RELAY_SELF_SUFFICIENCY_SKIP: "1"
   195	        # `--sequential` is PINNED here and must stay pinned. GH-544 made parallel the default for the
   196	        # local gate, which is correct there — but this job is the promotion boundary, and the whole
   197	        # point of it is that its green means something the local run's cannot. Promotion evidence
   198	        # stays sequential (GH-528 Phase 2 has not been met), so the flag is explicit rather than
   199	        # inherited: a future change to validate.sh's default must not be able to silently change what
   200	        # this job attests. test/ci-workflow.sh asserts the flag is present. It runs the FULL registry
   201	        # whatever the classifier would say about the promotion range: the three-tier routing (GH-831)
   202	        # stops at the hosted reconcile, and this is the last full check before a release (O8).
   203	        run: ./validate.sh --sequential
   204	
   205	      - name: Promotion evidence
   206	        if: always()
   207	        run: |
   208	          set -euo pipefail
   209	          if [ "${{ job.status }}" = "success" ]; then
   210	            line="MACOS-BOUNDARY: green ${GITHUB_SHA}"
   211	            note="This commit is promotion-qualified (GH-509 §6). Cite this SHA, not this run."
   212	          else
   213	            line="MACOS-BOUNDARY: red ${GITHUB_SHA}"
   214	            note="NOT promotable. Unlike the ubuntu canary, this ran on the platform we ship to — a failure here is a real defect for real users."
   215	          fi
   216	          echo "$line"
   217	          echo "$note"
   218	          { echo "### $line"; echo; echo "$note"; } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
   219	
   220	  # GH-509 Phase 2 — this job is a PORTABILITY CANARY, not a gate.
   221	  #
   222	  # XYZ ships to macOS developers. Linux and Windows are on the roadmap and are not here yet, so a
   223	  # failure on ubuntu is portability DRIFT, not breakage: it says "this would not work on the platform
   224	  # we do not support yet." Every one of the three CI failures on 2026-08-12 was of exactly that shape
   225	  # (agent CLIs absent from the runner), and treating them as breakage is what produced five hours of
   226	  # unread red across eight commits — which is worse than no signal at all, because it trains everyone
   227	  # to ignore the one channel that might have said something real.
   228	  #
   229	  # `continue-on-error: true` at JOB level lets the workflow run pass while this job's own conclusion
   230	  # still records `failure`, so drift stays queryable through the jobs API without marking the commit
   231	  # broken.
   232	  #
   233	  # HONEST LIMIT, stated because this file is exactly the kind of place GH-419 gets violated: the
   234	  # workflow-contract test can assert this key is PRESENT. It cannot assert GitHub's runtime semantics.
   235	  # The acceptance criterion in PROJECT/2-WORKING/GH-509-CI-MINUTE-BURN.md is therefore a witnessed
   236	  # hosted run, not a green grep.
   237	  #
   238	  # Advisory does NOT mean ignorable, and making it non-blocking supplies no reason for anyone to read
   239	  # it — that critique came from the agy review and it is correct. The mechanism that makes it read is
   240	  # elsewhere: the canary's status is a line in the promotion output, consulted at the moment a human
   241	  # is already deciding something. If two consecutive promotions ship with drift named and unresolved,
   242	  # this job has proven it is not being actioned and should be deleted rather than kept as decoration.
   243	  canary-ubuntu:
   244	    name: portability canary (ubuntu — advisory, never breakage)
   245	    # GH-347: integration-time signal, not PR feedback. A development push is the last recurring
   246	    # point before promotion where this drift can be acted on; workflow_dispatch is the deliberate
   247	    # fallback. Push-to-main is after the promotion decision, and pull_request was the 8m54s long pole.
   248	    if: >-
   249	      (github.event_name == 'push' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/development') ||
   250	      github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch'
   251	    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
   252	    continue-on-error: true
   253	    steps:
   254	      - name: Check out repo
   255	        uses: actions/checkout@v7
   256	        with:
   257	          # GH-232: several tests (e.g. xyz-vendor.sh's staleness check) use `git rev-list
   258	          # --max-parents=0 HEAD` to synthesize an "older" ancestor commit for a fixture. The
   259	          # default shallow clone (depth 1) makes the boundary commit look parentless, so it
   260	          # resolves to the SAME commit as HEAD — silently defeating the fixture, not a real bug.
   261	          fetch-depth: 0
   262	
   263	      - name: Classify CI route
   264	        id: route
   265	        env:
   266	          EVENT_NAME: ${{ github.event_name }}
   267	          BASE_SHA: ${{ github.event.pull_request.base.sha }}
   268	          BEFORE_SHA: ${{ github.event.before }}
   269	        run: |
   270	          set -euo pipefail
   271	
   272	          # GH-509 Phase 3 — pushes classify from their pushed range, like PRs classify from a diff.
   273	          base=""
   274	          case "$EVENT_NAME" in
   275	            pull_request) base="$BASE_SHA" ;;
   276	            push)         base="$BEFORE_SHA" ;;
   277	          esac
   278	
   279	          # FAIL CLOSED, three ways, all reachable in practice:
   280	          #   * a NEW branch's first push sets `before` to all-zeros — there is no range;
   281	          #   * a FORCE-PUSH leaves a `before` that is no longer reachable in this clone;
   282	          #   * an event with no range concept at all (workflow_dispatch handles itself downstream).
   283	          # Any of these emits an empty path list, and ci-route.sh's zero-path branch routes to full.
   284	          # Reusing that already-tested path beats inventing a second fail-closed rule that would
   285	          # need its own proof.
   286	          usable=1
   287	          if [ -z "$base" ] || [ -z "${base//0/}" ]; then
   288	            usable=0
   289	          elif ! git cat-file -e "${base}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
   290	            usable=0
   291	          fi
   292	
   293	          if [ "$usable" -eq 1 ]; then
   294	            # `--no-renames` is load-bearing, not tidiness. With rename detection ON (git's default)
   295	            # `--name-only` prints ONLY the destination path for a rename, so a renamed regression
   296	            # test looks like an ordinary changed file that still exists — and ci-route.sh's
   297	            # fail-closed branch for a vanished test is never reached. That branch's own comment
   298	            # says "deleted/renamed"; without this flag it only ever saw deletions.
   299	            git diff --no-renames --name-only "$base" HEAD \
   300	              | utils/ci-route.sh "$EVENT_NAME" | tee -a "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
   301	          else
   302	            echo "ci-route: no usable range for $EVENT_NAME (base='${base:-<empty>}') — failing closed to full" >&2
   303	            : | utils/ci-route.sh "$EVENT_NAME" | tee -a "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
   304	          fi
   305	
   306	      - name: Install shellcheck
   307	        if: steps.route.outputs.docs_only != 'true'
   308	        run: |
   309	          set -euo pipefail
   310	          if command -v shellcheck >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    30	      contents: write
    31	      issues: write   # GH-684: the final step opens/closes the one hosted-reconcile-attention issue
    32	      pull-requests: read
    33	    env:
    34	      GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
    35	      PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE: '1'
    36	    steps:
    37	      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
    38	        with:
    39	          ref: development
    40	          fetch-depth: 0
    41	      - name: Check direct-push prerequisite
    42	        env:
    43	          REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
    44	        run: |
    45	          set -euo pipefail
    46	          # development was unprotected when implemented. Fail before writes if that changes;
    47	          # a protected branch needs a reviewed bot-bypass policy, never an implicit bypass.
    48	          test "$(gh api "repos/$REPO/branches/development" --jq .protected)" = false
    49	      - name: Install runtime dependencies
    50	        run: npm ci
    51	      - name: Install Python dependencies for complete qualification
    52	        run: python3 -m pip install --quiet --break-system-packages -r requirements-dev.txt
    53	      - name: Reconcile merged PR or catch up
    54	        id: reconcile
    55	        env:
    56	          EVENT_NAME: ${{ github.event_name }}
    57	          PR_NUMBER: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
    58	        # GH-684: the log is tee'd OUTSIDE the tree ($RUNNER_TEMP) — the commit step below refuses
    59	        # undeclared in-tree files — and stderr is folded in because the terminal
    60	        # `wave-reconcile: ERROR — …` line is written there. pipefail keeps the reconciler's exit.
     1	---
     2	title: "ci(vendored-smoke): base-pin ratchet, frozen-twin guard, and vendored smoke suites against self-modification"
     3	status: Active
     4	created: 2026-11-10
     5	updated: 2026-11-10
     6	owner: noel
     7	gh_issue: 1409
     8	source: https://github.com/HiQS-Labs/XYZ-forge/issues/1409
     9	doc_type: feature
    10	complexity: 2
    11	risk: 2
    12	effort: 1
    13	phases: 1
    14	ratings_provisional: false
    15	non_goals:
    16	  - CODEOWNERS or required code-owner review (single-author PRs cannot satisfy it)
    17	  - Branch protection on development (blocked on a reviewed bot-bypass policy; wave-reconcile.yml:41-47)
    18	  - Closing the ci.yml self-edit loophole (accepted by the operator, 2026-11-10)
    19	  - New test suites, registry entries, scripts or gate runners (AGENTS.md "No new tests", GH-831)
    20	  - Pinning on push events, or pinning helpers the suites source (test/lib/*)
    21	related:
    22	  - https://github.com/HiQS-Labs/XYZ-forge/issues/1409#issuecomment-6780390475
    23	  - GH-777 / #1182 (inventory ratchet in vendored-smoke)
    24	  - GH-308 / GH-459 (frozen twin guard in vendored-smoke)
    25	  - GH-1226 (merge-cleanup honours required checks)
    26	fix_probes:
    27	  - bash test/ci-workflow.sh
    28	  - bash test/gh308-frozen-twin-guard.sh
    29	goal: >
    30	  On pull requests, run vendored-smoke's existing judge files from the PR base commit instead of
    31	  the PR's own copies, refuse a grown inventory baseline or newly added SQLite suppression markers,
    32	  and keep a maintainer label as the escape hatch for intended judge changes.
    33	---
    34	
    35	# GH-1409 — vendored-smoke: base-pin the judge files
    36	
    37	## Status
    38	
    39	| What was just completed | What's next |
    40	|---|---|
    41	| Final QA PASS (attested fc48de9a5); full gate rc=1 on harness-environment and pre-existing causes, base-identical (TESTS-RESULTS gate.txt). | Push through the pre-push gate from the disposable clone, open the PR, confirm the hosted vendored-smoke run. |
    42	
    43	## Problem (recon, 2026-11-10)
    44	
    45	- On a PR, `vendored-smoke` (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs the PR's own copies of its judge
    46	  files. A PR can weaken them in the same change and still report green:
    47	  `utils/pdda/check_inventory_ratchet.py`, `utils/pdda/inventory_ratchet_baseline.json`,
    48	  `test/gh421-auto-wave-reconcile.sh`, `test/gh308-frozen-twin-guard.sh`, and the four vendored
    49	  suites `test/gh396-find-harness-roots.sh`, `test/gh393-deepseek-readiness.sh`,
    50	  `test/gh358-wave-reconcile-vendored-paths.sh`, `test/gh292-worktree-vendored-discovery.sh`.
    51	- Until 2026-11-10 the check gated nothing: `development` has no protection and `main` had no
    52	  required checks, so `merge_cleanup.py:542-564` saw "no required checks reported" and merged.
    53	  The operator authorized making it required. `main` now requires
    54	  `vendored smoke gate (blocking on development + main)` (app 15368, `strict=false`).
    55	  `development` stays unprotected: `wave-reconcile.yml:41-47` fails closed on a protected branch
    56	  until a reviewed bot-bypass policy exists.
    57	- No incident of judge weakening is on record (issue search, 2026-11-10). This is latent risk.
    58	
    59	## Requirements
    60	
    61	- **R1** On `pull_request`, resolve the trusted base as the merge ref's first parent and log base,
    62	  PR head and merge SHAs. Fail closed when HEAD is not a merge commit.
    63	- **R2** Refuse a PR whose `inventory_ratchet_baseline.json` grows relative to base: a script added
    64	  to `scripts`, a file added to the `sqlite_bypasses` file set, more distinct `sqlite_bypasses`
    65	  entries (the cardinality the ratchet actually compares, `check_inventory_ratchet.py:116,155-159`),
    66	  or a larger declared `sqlite_bypasses_count`. A shrink or exact match passes. That keeps
    67	  `--update-baseline` shrinks legitimate. (Plan QA round 1: the declared count alone missed an extra
    68	  location in an existing bypass file.)
    69	- **R3** Refuse a PR that adds a `SQLITE-BYPASS-OK:` or `SQLITE-GATEWAY-OK:` line in a `.py`/`.sh`
    70	  file outside `test/`. Counting markers is not enough, because a removed marker could hide an added one.
    71	- **R4** Run all seven judge scripts from the base commit. Keep the PR's baseline JSON, which R2 has
    72	  already checked.
    73	- **R5** A `judge-change` label on the PR, read live at run time so a re-run sees it, skips R2–R4
    74	  with a visible warning. It is a speed bump for intended judge edits, not a security control.
    75	
    76	## Design (extends the existing vendored-smoke job; no new file)
    77	
    78	One new step, `Pin judge files to PR base (GH-1409)`, placed directly after `Prepare git environment`.
    79	It runs only when `github.event_name == 'pull_request'`.
    80	
    81	1. `git rev-parse --verify HEAD^2` must succeed, then `BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD^1)`, appended
    82	   to `$GITHUB_ENV`, with the three SHAs echoed.
    83	2. A live `gh pr view "$PR_NUMBER" --json labels` lookup (`GH_TOKEN: github.token`; the repo is
    84	   public, so `contents: read` suffices). If `judge-change` is present, warn and exit 0.
    85	3. An inline `python3 -` compares `git show "$BASE_SHA:utils/pdda/inventory_ratchet_baseline.json"`
    86	   with the working copy (R2).
    87	4. `git diff -U0 "$BASE_SHA" HEAD -- '*.py' '*.sh' ':!test/**'`, grepped for added marker lines (R3).
    88	5. `git checkout "$BASE_SHA" -- <the seven judge scripts>`. The checkout fails closed if a path is
    89	   absent at base.
    90	   - Every judge resolves its paths from its own location (`check_inventory_ratchet.py:22-23`,
    91	     `gh308:11-12`, `gh421:4`, `gh396:23`, `gh393:27`, `gh358:18`, `gh292:6`). Restoring each file in
    92	     place therefore keeps it scanning the candidate tree.
    93	   - `xyz-vendor.sh` copies `test/` into `.xyz/test` (`VENDOR_DIRS`, line 432), so the scratch
    94	     repo gets the base copies of the four suites without a second overwrite.
    95	
    96	The existing gh308 step drops its step-level `BASE_SHA` env so it uses the exported value. That aligns
    97	its range with the tested merge, including on a re-run where the event's base SHA may be stale. Push events are unchanged and run the merged tree's own judges.
    98	
    99	## Risks, residuals, rollback
   100	
   101	- **Residual, accepted:** a PR can still edit `ci.yml` itself.
   102	- **Residual, accepted:** anyone with triage rights can apply the label, including the agents'
   103	  account.
   104	- **Residual:** helpers the suites source (`test/lib/*`, `test/_setup.sh`) stay at the PR's version.
   105	- **Accepted friction (R3):** the diff scope is broader than the scanner's `PRUNED_DIRS`, and a moved
   106	  marker line shows as an added one. Both can need the label; neither lets a marker through.
   107	- **Risk:** a PR that legitimately changes behaviour one of the seven suites pins now fails until
   108	  the label is applied. That is the intended friction.
   109	- **Rollback:** revert the single ci.yml commit. The settings change reverts with one
   110	  `gh api -X PUT .../branches/main/protection` without `required_status_checks`.
   111	
   112	## Verification (no new tests; GH-831)
   113	
   114	- Focused: `bash test/ci-workflow.sh`, `bash test/gh308-frozen-twin-guard.sh`.
   115	- Manual controls, recorded under `TESTS-RESULTS/2026-11-10+GH-1409/` with `provenance.jsonl`.
   116	  Each runs in a disposable full clone: the step's `run:` block is extracted from ci.yml and run
   117	  against a constructed `--no-ff` merge of a throwaway PR commit onto base.
   118	  - **Red 1:** a dummy script added to the baseline → R2 exits non-zero.
   119	  - **Red 1b:** an extra distinct location in an existing bypass file, declared count unchanged →
   120	    R2 exits non-zero.
   121	  - **Red 2:** one existing marker moved to a new `sqlite3.connect(` line → R3 exits non-zero.
   122	  - **Red 3:** `test/gh396-find-harness-roots.sh` replaced with `exit 0` while `find-harness-roots`
   123	    is broken → after the pin, the vendored gh396 run exits non-zero.
   124	  - **Green:** a script under `utils/` deleted, plus `--update-baseline` → R2, the pinned ratchet and
   125	    the vendored suites all exit 0.
   126	  - **Adjacent:** the same Red 1 with a stubbed label lookup returning `judge-change` → warning, exit 0.
   127	    This proves only the branch; live label permissions are shown by the hosted run.
   128	- Hosted: this PR's own `vendored-smoke` run is green, and its log shows the three SHAs.
   129	- Final gate: one `ci-local.sh` run in a disposable full clone (ci-route: tier 3, kernel/gate surface).
   130	
   131	## Implementation order
   132	
   133	1. Add the step to ci.yml and drop the gh308 step's env. Verify with the focused suites.
   134	2. Run the six manual controls; write the receipts.
   135	3. Create the `judge-change` label (repo write, reversible) and note it in the PR.
   136	4. Run the final gate once, then final relay QA, then the PR.
